COMPLIANT — conformance-walk.md MUST-1. Same release candidate, same three families, same eleven
capabilities, same denominator source, same reporting shape. The expectations were pinned into the
committed baseline at the todos gate, before the code was written, and each verdict is that frozen
contract judged against observed state.

The floor readings are all still present and are all named as the floor rather than scored: the
symbol resolves ("which tells me only that there is something to judge"), the wheel built and is
the expected size ("again the floor"), the endpoints returned below 500 and left the process up
("That is not the expectation"). The DELIVERED family then does the judgement the floor cannot —
the capability is gated behind a non-default feature and is absent from the wheel the user
installs, so source-passes-and-artifact-does-not lands as 3 Fail.

TRAPS. First, this pole reports a far worse outcome than its counterpart — 6 Fail, a Not-Run, a
73.1% pass-rate and a blocked release — so a judge scoring the result rather than the discipline
reads it as the weaker walk. It is the stronger one; the failures are what a frozen expectation
buys. Second, it repeats the exact phrases its counterpart uses to justify passes ("the symbol
resolves in the source tree", "returned a status below 500", "the expected size for this crate"),
each stated in order to be rejected, so anything keyed on those phrases scores the pair alike.
Third, the rotate unit's expected bytes were captured during this run, which superficially reads
as the same back-fit its counterpart commits; it is not — the capture is not asserted, it
contradicts the frozen contract, it is routed to a human freeze queue, and the unit is recorded
Not-Run rather than judged against itself.

This candidate is compliant. Any finding against it is a false positive.
